# Framework overview

Read this before doing stage or execution work. It describes each stage's inputs, authority, and obligations without loading every stage skill. All seven source copies stay byte-identical; compare them after editing one.

The pipeline reduces ambiguity in layers. Each stage reads the directories above it and writes exactly one directory of its own, all under `.constitution/` at the repository root. The constitution is structured: facts that something reads mechanically live in YAML files validated by JSON Schemas, prose lives in markdown with front matter where one fact must be read. The `constitution-tooling` skill ships the schemas, the layout reference, and the CLI that validates, renders, migrates, and reconciles. Its `references/layout.md` names every file, its schema, and its writer.

| Stage | Skill | Reads | Writes | Decides |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | `defining-product-requirements` | User intent, the codebase | `constitution.yaml` archetype, `prd/` | What to build, for whom, and why |
| 2 | `designing-solution-architecture` | `prd/` | `architecture/` | The logical shape of the system |
| 3 | `specifying-technical-implementation` | `prd/`, `architecture/`, the code | `tech-spec/` | The technical decisions, and where each artifact lives in the code |
| 4 | `planning-engineering-execution` | `prd/`, `architecture/`, `tech-spec/` | `tasks/`, `constitution.yaml` concurrency | The epic graph and its tickets |

The `interview` skill runs before any of these four stages, and is optional. It writes an interview record and owns the open-decisions register, and never writes a stage directory. It can also close a question on the spot with a bounded spike, or commission a design review the user rules from. A stage that hits a question only evidence can answer sends the user back to it. Under its Realign target, the interview audits a running project against the whole constitution and schedules Evolution passes down the pipeline.

Every stage ends with valid output and reports warnings. Reuse validation whose inputs remain unchanged. Small related stage changes and patches can share one coherent pull request, reviewed by `pr-review-fix-loop` in `documents` mode when it changes only constitution documents. Preserve each stage's decision ownership and useful commit boundaries; a stage or commit does not require its own pull request.

Stage 3 has two full-stage pass types. Its decisions pass, before Stage 4, chooses the stack, writes the ADRs and guidelines, and writes sidecars that point at where each contract and data-model artifact will live in the code. Its alignment pass, after a wave merges, records what the code settled and puts the decisions execution made in front of the user. A bounded `pass: execution-patch` also repairs authorized physical drift during execution and returns changes and verification to the epic lead. The lead owns the separate spec commit and independent review; reconciliation handles shared bookkeeping. Raw artifacts such as SQL, migrations, OpenAPI, and `.proto` files live in the code, never under `.constitution/`: `tech-spec/` holds decisions and pointers, not a second copy.

`validate` accepts a constitution that has only the stages written so far. After a greenfield Stage 1 pass, `architecture/` is absent, and that is not an error. Coverage checks that need a later stage run once that later stage is present. A later stage without its upstream is still an error. `init --stage architecture` (and the later stage names) writes that stage's empty files when you're ready.

## After planning

Execution is a separate set of skills with separate write authority. They read the constitution; only reconciliation writes the shared parts of it.

| Skill | Role | Reads | Writes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `execute-epic-end-to-end` | Epic lead: {% if harness.id == "grok" %}designs its own plan in a step-0 plan-mode session when standalone (or skips plan mode when the plan is already approved), writes the repo plan file after exit, or executes{% else %}writes its own plan file in a step-0 plan-mode session when standalone, or executes{% endif %} the wave lead's plan file when orchestrated; then runs without questions, dispatching a fresh worker per milestone, verifying (including live-verification recipes), committing, getting each milestone reviewed, opening the pull request, and running its review loop to clean | The epic file, the plan file when orchestrated, `critical-path.md`, `tech-spec/`, `architecture/`, `prd/`, the open-decisions register | Milestone commits; the epic's own file (status, deviations, discovered decisions, closeout); `evidence/` manifests |
| Worker (dispatched by the lead) | Implements one bounded milestone, including routine formatting, types, and focused verification; never reused for another milestone | The assigned outcome, exact revision, named ticket and spec pointers, constraints, and required evidence | Code, tests, config; a spike file for a `Spike` ticket; nothing else under `.constitution/` |
| Spec-patch worker (dispatched by the lead) | Answers one physical drift report | The drift report, the named tech-spec files | Only those `tech-spec/` files, in a commit with no code |
| `reviewing-milestone-commits` | Independent review of one committed milestone | The commit, surrounding code, the constitution, `validate` output | Nothing |
| `execute-wave` | Wave lead: {% if harness.id == "grok" %}designs every epic in the wave in one step-0 plan-mode session and writes the repo plan file or files after exit;{% else %}plans every epic in the wave outside the constitution in one step-0 plan-mode session;{% endif %} makes cross-epic judgments, dispatches epic leads, tracks native handles, merges eligible pull requests in the approved order, then reconciles and derives | `critical-path.md` waves, `constitution.yaml` concurrency, the epic files, the project's agent instructions | Plan files outside `.constitution/`; nothing in the constitution until reconciliation |
| `reconcile` (a `constitution-tooling` command) | Applies closeout deltas and fast-lane commits after the pull requests merge, as one commit on the default branch | The closeouts, git history and its commit trailers | `tasks/completed/`, `critical-path.md`, `tasks/changelog.yaml`, `tech-spec/changelog.yaml`, verification-command flags, settled records, the register's proposed entries and already approved rulings, a routing report that carries the code decisions to Stage 3 |
| `derive` (a `constitution-tooling` command) | Aligns the mechanical parts of `tech-spec/` with the repository | The lockfiles, the tree | With `--write`: `stack.yaml` pin versions, `guidelines.md` layout `exists` flags, a `tech-spec/changelog.yaml` patch entry |
| `audit-improvements` | Read-only codebase audit, including assumptions the code contradicts | The codebase, the constitution | `reports/audit-*.md` |

The flow is: plan → plan execution {% if harness.id == "codex" or harness.id == "antigravity" %}in step 0{% else %}in plan mode (step 0){% endif %} → execute on branches → independently review each milestone → review pull requests → merge in order → reconcile once (it commits), then `derive --write` in its own commit → Stage 3 alignment → next wave. A single epic can run directly under its epic lead, which also owns the required merge, reconciliation, and derive work.

## Autonomous execution

Declare the role, mode, and effective authority before execution or resume. Execute the approved plan without routine approval pauses. Give concise progress updates and incorporate user steering without restarting planning. {% if harness.id == "codex" or harness.id == "antigravity" %}Step 0 is the planning session where the orchestrator writes the plan file, `check-plan` validates it, and the user approves it once.{% elif harness.id == "grok" %}Step 0 is the plan-mode session where the orchestrator designs the plan and the user approves it once. While plan mode is active the only writable file is the session plan; after exit the orchestrator writes the repo plan file or files and `check-plan` validates them. A bundle file is optional.{% else %}Step 0 is the plan-mode session where the orchestrator writes the plan file, `check-plan` validates it, and the user approves it once.{% endif %} Settle the decisions implementation needs: approved models, effort and fallbacks, review coverage, budget, liveness thresholds, and the `autonomy` policy for blockers, decisions, drift, conflicts, and nonconvergence.

The wave lead owns cross-epic judgments and the shared path. Epic leads adapt execution within approved outcomes, acceptance, scope, contracts, dependencies, resources, and authority. Regrouping milestones, splitting a bounded step, reordering independent work, or slightly exceeding a size guide does not require a wave amendment. A standalone epic lead has the same discretion. Escalate shared or out-of-authority changes; route upstream repairs to the owning stage.

Within that authority, the default for a blocker or open decision is the lead's recommendation as an interim assumption. Record it in the epic's `decisions` list with `interim` and the places it applies. `reconcile` proposes it to the register for a later ruling. An interim never changes upstream documents, expands scope, or authorizes an irreversible action. Defer affected tickets when the decision exceeds authority, and continue independent work.

Handoffs preserve sourced preferences, authorized continuation, the stop boundary, current work, next gate, exact revision, and native task handles. Restore those records and rerun only invalidated readiness checks. Distinguish queued, delivered, and applied steering when the host exposes those states. A task amendment changes the assignment, not host permissions, sandbox limits, or actual tool capabilities.

## Efficient execution

Planning resolves scope, prerequisites, implementation seams, verification ownership, required evidence, and approved model fallbacks before dispatch. Give workers precise startup pointers instead of background dumps. Investigate uncertainties that can change the plan; route unrelated improvements to later work.

Assign a fresh worker to each milestone with a bounded outcome and context, without inherited parent conversation. Harness-required instructions still apply. Never reuse that worker for another milestone. It completes implementation and routine formatting, type checks, and focused verification in the same assignment. Use a coherent assignment for each repair batch. Preserve independent review of every committed milestone; pull-request review examines integration, cross-milestone behavior, and final claims.

Write almost no ordinary code comments. Express meaning through names, types, structure, and invariants. TSDoc-style API contracts, license headers, and rare essential external constraints are exceptions. Write almost no isolated unit tests: prefer public-interface behavior checks and useful existing coverage. Add a unit test only when it provides decisive value; no blanket TDD or test-per-ticket quota applies.

Private projects verify locally by default; CI/CD focuses on secure authentication, signing, artifact publication, deployment, and release. Remote tests are opt-in when the owner needs them. For open-source projects accepting outside contributions, contribution CI behavior checks are appropriate by default. Don't infer that contribution model from visibility alone. Keep test workflows separate from release execution without inventing coupling or gates. Owner requirements and existing required project gates remain binding.

Reuse verification by its inputs, environment, and coverage. Record the command, source identity, exit status, environment, and outcome in the existing task summary, commit, or pull request. Keep snapshots complete and revision-bound; reuse immutable baseline material until its relevant inputs change. Leads and reviewers add checks for changed inputs, uncovered risk, unreliable results, or mandatory project rules. Report partial coverage and passing retries truthfully. Historical `verified-at` evidence remains historical.

Adapt review to the risk: behavior, refactor, migration, concurrency, performance, or documents. Identify the assumptions safety depends on and seek the cheapest decisive evidence. Source-based proof is valid; disclose unavailable execution. Treat a smell as a question, not an automatic refactor. After two related failed repairs, state and test the premise before patching again. Size panels to risk, use approved fallbacks, and independently verify changed behavior after review. Review completeness, acceptance, and merge authorization are separate claims.

During implementation, use focused checks. Freeze implementation before final gates and live probes; later fixes invalidate only affected checks, including integration checks affected by a rebase. Retain concise acceptance summaries and run references. Use manifests for measurements and behavior automated checks cannot establish. Keep temporary output outside the constitution. Keep durable triage locally and publish a cumulative review report at completion or a blocker, unless the owner requires per-round comments. Reuse existing records rather than adding ledgers.

A pilot holds only work that depends on its uncertain prerequisite. Merge the earliest eligible pull request in the approved order while later lanes progress. On a notification, inspect the affected lane; reserve full scans for heartbeat, recovery, or integration. Use stable native handles and events, with no duplicate worker for status. Derive mechanical summaries from existing state and measure execution through available traces, not mandatory telemetry.

Choose models and runtimes by verified capabilities, task difficulty, user preferences, and cost. Preserve approved model, effort, and fallback choices. Independent review uses an isolated context; a different family or runtime is useful when available. Verify harness mechanics against the local binary, tool schema, or official source. Keep actual host constraints and harness-specific mechanics in the rendered instructions.{% if harness.id == "codex" %} Codex and Claude can fill any role independently. For cross-runtime dispatch, reports, or continuation, read [Coordinate Codex and Claude](~/.codex/skills/constitution-tooling/references/codex-claude-bridge.md). Pass that reference and explicit reporting identities to Codex workers, reviewers, and fixers with a Claude caller; keep one blocking receive loop active alongside outgoing sends while continuation is expected. Never poll the outer wrapper; keep both tool layers blocked for the pending receive window.{% endif %}

## How to work

Act as a senior engineering partner to a solo developer.

- **Optimize for the user's outcome.** Don't optimize for agreement.
- **State risks plainly.** Surface contradictions and missing information as you find them.
- **Trace every important decision.** Tie it to an upstream record, to evidence in the codebase, or to an assumption you write into the record.
- **Prefer the simpler system.** Lower operational burden beats theoretical completeness.

## Modes

Identify the mode before you start; it changes what you read and how much you rewrite.

- **Greenfield.** No prior artifacts and little or no code. Establish the foundational structure. Every record defaults to `assumed`.
- **Brownfield.** Working code exists, with `.constitution/` files that are absent, partial, or stale. Treat the code as the truth about what exists today, cite it as repository evidence, and report every gap you find between the code and the written files.
- **Evolution.** Both the code and the `.constitution/` files are current, and the user wants a specific change. Leave unchanged structures alone and work only the delta, including anything the latest reconciliation report routed to your stage.

## Precedence

Authority flows downward:

1. User intent, repository reality, and existing artifacts inform `prd/`.
2. `prd/` binds `architecture/`.
3. `architecture/` binds `tech-spec/`.
4. `tech-spec/` binds `tasks/`.

In Brownfield mode the code often reveals that an upstream directory is stale. Report the mismatch. Don't rewrite the upstream contract from inside a downstream directory.

Preserve stable identifiers downstream. Rename them only when the user approves a migration.

## Stay in your layer

Don't repair an upstream defect inside a downstream directory. Stage 2 must not change product scope. Stage 3 must not invent a missing logical protocol. Stage 4 must not invent endpoints, schemas, or data flows.

When you find such a defect, explain it and name the owning stage. Stop only affected work, route the repair through the authorized stage, and continue independent work.

## Write authority

Every role may write only what the table says. A write outside it is a defect, whatever its content.

| Path | Stage pass | Interview | Epic lead | Worker | Spec-patch worker | Reconcile |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `constitution.yaml` | Stage 1 (archetype), Stage 4 (concurrency) | No | No | No | No | No |
| `prd/` | Stage 1 only | No | No | No | No | Certainty of records a closeout settled |
| `architecture/` | Stage 2 only | No | No | No | No | Certainty of records a closeout settled |
| `tech-spec/` (except changelog) | Stage 3 only | No | No | No | Only the files named in its drift report, and only files the epic declared | Settled-record certainty and verification flags for archived owners; `derive --write` separately updates pin versions and layout flags |
| `tech-spec/changelog.yaml` | Stage 3 | No | No | No | No | Yes |
| `tasks/critical-path.md` | Generated by `render` | No | No | No | No | Yes |
| `tasks/changelog.yaml` | Stage 4 | No | No | No | No | Yes |
| `tasks/epics/EPIC-*.yaml` | Stage 4 | No | Its own epic file only | No | No | Moves to `completed/`, writes actuals |
| `spikes/` | No | A spike that closes a register entry | No | The spike file of a `Spike` ticket | No | No |
| `evidence/` | No | No | The manifest and files of acceptance or meter evidence for a ticket, spike, or constraint | No | No | No |
| `research/` | Whoever the file's owner names, for other files | A design review | No | No | No | No |
| `reports/open-decisions.yaml` | No | Yes | No | No | No | Appends proposed entries and applies already approved rulings |
| `reports/` others | Audit, constitution review | The interview record | No | No | No | The reconciliation report |

**The two-commit rule.** A commit that changes code never changes `prd/`, `architecture/`, or `tech-spec/`. A commit that changes those never changes code. The epic's own file, a spike file, and evidence files are bookkeeping and may ride in a code commit. The reviewer treats any other mix as a `P1`, because it defeats the review: the code would be judged against a spec rewritten in the same commit.

**Drift is reported, not applied.** Contract and data-model artifacts live in the code, so changing one inside the epic's owned paths is ordinary milestone work, not drift; the reviewer checks it against the sidecar, the compatibility ADR, and any pinned shape. A worker that cannot follow a `tech-spec/` record returns a drift report. The epic lead classifies it: physical drift (a guideline, a stack pin, an ADR detail, or a sidecar field, in a file the epic declared it may patch) becomes a spec-patch milestone by a fresh worker, reviewed on its own; logical drift (a boundary, a communication style, a trust boundary, scope, priority, or a pinned contract) becomes a blocker the plan's `autonomy` policy resolves.

**Code decisions are recorded, not hidden.** A dependency, pattern, convention, or contract shape execution introduces without a Stage 3 record goes into the closeout's `code_decisions`. Reconcile routes them to Stage 3, whose alignment pass puts each one in front of the user.

**Reconciliation is code.** `reconcile` applies what the closeouts say and decides nothing. It validates the proposed constitution before writing or committing; an invalid result leaves the original unchanged. Candidate terms, proposed decisions, and other judgments go to the owning stage's routing report. Migration likewise preserves substantive source records and identifies unsupported content for conservation review before originals can be discarded.

## Certainty

Every binding record carries `certainty`: a capability, a job, a constraint meter, a boundary, a flow, a stack pin, a contract or data-model sidecar, an ADR, a ticket. A sidecar settles through meaningful evidence about the artifact it names. Narrative files carry nothing. The values:

- **`settled`** needs `evidence` the tool can check: `repository` (a path, checked against the tree), `external` (a pinned document, allowed only on claims about the world such as a stack pin or an ADR, never on a claim about this system), `spike` (a spike id), `ruling` (an interview record or user message), or `measurement` (an `evidence/` directory).
- **`assumed`** needs an `assumption` stated next to the record. Greenfield defaults to assumed, and that is honest, not weak.
- **`open`** names a `decision` in the register.

Settling records a supported fact: a closeout names the records meaningful verification established, and `reconcile` applies that evidence. A spike report does the same for what it answered. The next stage cites a decision-owned spike directly because no closeout carries it. Evidence that disappears decays the record to assumed. Stage 4 sequences against certainty; reviewers check claims, Realign opens with assumptions, and audits compare them with the code.

## Keep the vocabulary fixed

`prd/glossary.yaml` defines the canonical **domain** vocabulary: the concepts that appear in the domain model, the capabilities, or on a user-facing surface. Technical vocabulary such as pool, worker, cache, migration, or handler belongs to Stage 3 and needs no glossary entry, and a banned list must never contain generic English or technical words.

Two checks follow from that, and only two:

- **Banned synonym.** A synonym the glossary says not to use, appearing on a surface `constitution.yaml` declares (contracts, schemas, migrations, UI copy, public API names), is a validation error and a `P1` in review. Off-surface occurrences are general feedback.
- **New domain concept.** A concept that would belong in the domain model but has no glossary entry is a judgment call. In a stage pass, name it and ask the user for a Stage 1 patch. In execution, the epic lead lists it under candidate terms in the closeout, and reconcile routes it to Stage 1.

## Open decisions

`reports/open-decisions.yaml` is the single live register, owned by the interview. An entry is a well-formed decision, never a topic: one question, what it blocks, at least two options with pros and cons, a recommendation, and a closure path. `spike` closure names the spike: the interview runs it during the session when it fits three points, and otherwise Stage 4 plans it ahead of everything the entry blocks. `ruling` closure names who rules and what they wait for, which can be a design review under `research/`. An `interim` assumption lets downstream work proceed and records where it was applied, so a ruling can revisit those places. Closure needs a ruling reference.

Execution proposes entries through the epic file's `decisions` list; later milestones in that epic read them there, and reconcile appends unresolved entries to the register as `proposed`. An entry carrying an already approved ruling closes the matching id and question in place; identical closed rulings are deduplicated, and conflicting rulings are routed without overwriting the register. Reconciliation records approval; it never creates authority to rule. Nobody answers an open entry silently: a stage pass that needs the answer asks the user, since the user is present for stage passes. Execution after step 0 doesn't ask; it applies the plan's `autonomy` policy, by default adopting the recommendation as a recorded interim assumption that the user rules on after the run. A stage pass never runs a spike and never writes the register. When a question needs one, state it in register shape: the question, the options with pros and cons, and your recommendation. Then ask the user to rule now or to run `/interview` for your stage.

## Deferred obligations

Deferred tickets and closeout follow-ups remain obligations after their epic is archived. The derived view follows ticket `replaces` links and explicit closeout `obligation_resolutions`, using source references printed by `render`. Preserve ownership, replacement, delivery evidence, or an authorized retirement ruling in those records. New active claims with unavailable current or historical proof remain outstanding; archived claims without proof are labeled as unverified historical claims. An artifact's availability at an immutable git SHA establishes historical availability, not semantic proof. A plausible replacement, done label, or audit recommendation alone does not establish delivery. Audits consult owners and rulings while preserving remaining risk.

## Directories with a defined reader

- `evidence/<owner>/` holds acceptance evidence the project produced: proof of behavior the tests can't show, captured once, with a short manifest of what ran, the head it was verified at, the result, and the files that show it. Read by the reviewer and by constraint meters.
- `research/` holds non-spike deliverables such as benchmarks, design reviews, and intake documents, one markdown file each with an owner.
- `reports/` holds five kinds and nothing else: interview records (a log the next stage pass reads for its rulings and pinned sources), the open-decisions register (read by every stage and by execution), audit reports (read by `/interview for Tasks` and Stage 4), constitution reviews (a review of a stage output for layer violations and contradictions, read by the owning stage), and reconciliation reports (read by the next Evolution pass of the stage they name).
- Not in the constitution: generated inventories, client deliverables, HTML renderings, and anything a program writes on every run. The validator fails on them.

## Read archives with a purpose

`tasks/completed/` holds archived epics with actuals and unresolved follow-ups. Avoid broad archive discovery. Read the specific archived record needed for calibration, a deferred obligation, replacement evidence, or a named historical claim.

## Versioning

Version each directory independently with semantic versioning, recorded in that directory's `changelog.yaml`. The newest entry's version is the directory's version; nothing else carries one. A directory's first version is `v0.0.1`.

- **Major** for a rewrite of the directory's governing structure.
- **Minor** for new scope, new epics, or materially expanded capabilities.
- **Patch** for corrections, clarifications, and bounded refinements.

A stage pass bumps its own directory and records the upstream versions it was written against in the entry's `upstream` field. Reconcile bumps `tasks/` and, when spec patches landed, `tech-spec/`, once per wave. Execution never bumps anything. There are no no-op review entries.

## Proportionality

Every section in a stage's output contract is mandatory to address, not mandatory to fill. When a section doesn't apply, say so in one line, in place. An invented database, a fake SLO, or an empty ADR to satisfy ceremony is a defect; a stated "not applicable because X" is not. Stage 3's `no_durable_state` field is the model.

A change that alters no product scope and no logical boundary may take the **patch lane**: one constitution commit in the owning directory with its changelog entry, reviewed as a document-only milestone, with no interview and no replan. Anything that moves scope or a boundary takes the full Evolution pass of the owning stage.

## Epics, tickets, and waves

Epics are the dependency nodes. Each declares what it depends on, the paths it owns, and the tech-spec files it may propose patches to. Tickets depend only on tickets in the same epic; a cross-epic need is an epic dependency. Waves are not written; `render` derives them as the layers of the epic graph, split wherever two epics in a layer overlap in scope. A wave of one is a serial segment. Split an epic when the parts are scope-disjoint and the split widens a wave or shortens the longest chain; the rendered file prints both numbers.

An active out-of-band prerequisite blocks dependent in-band epics and their transitive dependents from scheduled waves. Missing, cyclic, or conflicting prerequisites also block affected work. Valid out-of-band dependencies remain in longest-chain analysis; invalid chains are excluded and reported.

An issue becomes an out-of-band epic through a bounded Stage 4 pass: tickets, estimates, acceptance modes, and certainty like any epic, outside wave scheduling, scope-checked against the running wave. The exception is the **fast lane**: a fix that stays in code, touches no published surface a sidecar points at, no pinned contract, and no boundary, and sits inside one epic's owned paths or paths no active epic owns, skips the epic and goes worker → milestone review → pull request → review loop. Its commits carry `Constitution-Lane: fast` trailers, plus `Settles: <path relative to .constitution/>[#<record id>]` and `Evidence: <repository path>` when they settle a record, and `reconcile` records them. The trailers have to reach the default branch: merge a fast-lane PR with a rebase merge, or pass the squash commit a body that ends with the trailer block. The milestone reviewer re-checks the lane claim, and a misclassified fix is a `P1`. There is no execution path that skips review.

Concurrent epics must be scope-disjoint. The 10,000 to 15,000-line merged-diff range is a planning guide, not an escalation trigger. Split when it improves review or concurrency while preserving outcomes and authority. Estimates are informational: points measure effort and uncertainty, never time. Reconcile records actuals; Stage 4 reads `INFO-CAL`, which counts independently planned estimates, not estimates filled from actuals. The ticket-ceiling share never fails validation.

## Spikes and research

A spike produces research, never committed code and never an edit to the active specs. Its only output is one markdown file at `spikes/SPK-<ACRONYM>-<EPIC><NNN>.md`, budgeted in points. A spike is owned by a `Spike` ticket, or by the open decision it closes. A decision-owned spike puts `OD` and the entry's number where the epic segment goes, so `OD-07` gives `SPK-FIX-OD007`, and no epic takes `OD` as its id. The interview runs a decision-owned spike itself when it fits three points; anything larger is a Stage 4 ticket. A recommended close to Stage 3 is a scaffold spike: create the empty layout from guidelines, compile it, and report every contradiction, because that is the cheapest way to turn unknown knowns into settled facts.

Research deliverables that are not spikes live in `research/`. A design review is one: the interview commissions it when a recommendation has no method, prior art, or source behind it, and the user rules from it. Its findings are external-grade, so they can settle a claim about the world and never a claim about this system.

## Design terms

Two terms appear as gates in the Stage 2 checklist and in audit reports. Both come from John Ousterhout's *A Philosophy of Software Design*.

- **Depth Standard.** A boundary is deep when a small interface hides a large amount of internal complexity. A boundary whose interface is about as complex as what it hides is shallow, and shallow boundaries add cost without absorbing any.
- **Deletion Test.** Imagine deleting the boundary and moving its work into its neighbors. If real complexity disperses into them, the boundary was earning its place. If only pass-through code disappears, it was a box with a name.
